#
# The sign-in stack of the Appliance Console, installed at /etc/pam.d/olivares-portal, the
# file the service name selects. It exists for exactly one question, asked only while the
# product is down: who is this human logging in to this host. It is never a session
# identity and never a provider-account identity, and the console holds no credential of
# its own — this stack, and the host's own scheme inside it, is the only thing here that
# ever sees one.
#
# WHY THE FILE MUST EXIST AT ALL, and why the console treats it as unusable when it does
# not. A service with no file of its own does not fail closed on a Debian host: the
# fallback stack includes the host's common authentication scheme, so an absent file would
# admit every local account instead of admitting nobody. The console therefore refuses over
# the network when this stack cannot be read, and says that the remaining door is the
# repair console on tty1 — which is the only surface that can repair this file. (On AlmaLinux the
# fallback denies every group, so an absent file admits nobody there; the console treats
# absence as unusable on every host.)
#
# THIS FILE IS THE DEB'S. It includes Debian's common-auth and common-account. The rpm installs
# rpm/olivares-portal at the same path: the same stack over AlmaLinux's password-auth.
#
# THE GROUP CHECK IS FIRST, AND IT IS REQUISITE. A human the host does not place in the
# named local administrators group is refused before the auth group reaches the host's
# scheme, so their credential is never asked for and never checked. The console performs
# the same check itself, for the case this file is replaced; neither check is a policy
# decision, because group membership is the host's own record of who administers it.
#
# THE SESSION AND PASSWORD GROUPS ARE DENIED OUTRIGHT. The console opens no session and
# changes no credential, so the two groups that would do either are closed here rather
# than left to a caller's good manners.
#
# MEASURED AGAINST A REAL STACK, in a container with this file installed and real accounts: on
# Debian 12 with this file, and on AlmaLinux 10.2 (pam 1.6.1) with the rpm file, a
# member of olivares-admins with the right password is authenticated and its account allowed; a member
# with an expired account is authenticated and its account refused; a non-member (right or wrong
# password), a locked password, a wrong password, an unknown login and root outside the group are
# refused. The same service name with no file of its own admits a non-member on Debian and nobody on
# AlmaLinux, which is why the console treats an absent file as unusable. Session opening and an
# enforcing SELinux host are not covered by that measurement. The layer's own tests read this file's
# directives and their order.
auth       requisite   pam_succeed_if.so user ingroup olivares-admins
auth       include     common-auth
account    required    pam_succeed_if.so user ingroup olivares-admins
account    include     common-account
password   required    pam_deny.so
session    required    pam_deny.so
